Coronapomp
Ingredients: SYNTHPOP & NEW ROMANTICS + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Double Bass
Creative Catalyst: Descends from carnival musicians who perform only during solar eclipses.

Coronapomp is a whirlwind of theatrical, high-stepping daylight hysteria. It fuses the aristocratic melodrama, shimmering sequined synthesizers, and flamboyant vocal posturing of the New Romantic movement with the ecstatic, runaway locomotive velocity of an unhinged carnival parade. At its kinetic core sits an acoustic double bass slapped with violent, joyful precision, ricocheting beneath towering brass-synth fanfares and jubilant multi-part operatic choruses that celebrate sudden, fleeting astronomical darkness with uncontainable joy.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 138–156 BPM. A galloping, syncopated stride-polka pulse driven by frantic swung high-hats, handclaps, and rhythmic, slap-back double bass pizzicato that treats every measure like a high-wire sprint.
- Harmonic Palette: Sparkling Lydian and Mixolydian modes; dramatic major-sixth fanfares, Pierrot-esque theatrical minor-to-major key modulations, and soaring four-part vocal polyphony colored by baroque court flourishes.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Optical Lux-Gate Processing: An impossible analog production technique where synth filter cuts and tape-delay feedback loops are modulated in real time by photo-reactive selenium cells trained on the dimming light of a solar eclipse, causing the bright synth pads to bloom into fizzy, overdriven warmth as the midday sun disappears.
  2. Spruce-and-Gut Slap Transduction: Contact microphones mounted directly into the bridge and fingerboard of an upright double bass, blending the dry, woody percussive click of gut strings against maple with the massive low-end sine burst of an analog drum voice.
  3. Unfiltered Calliope-Stacking: Vintage polyphonic synthesizers (Roland Jupiter-8 and Prophet-5) tracked through vintage mechanical carnival calliope pipes, resulting in glistening, whistling synth chords with an airy, brassy wheeze.
- Dynamics: Hyper-kinetic and explosive. Tracks open with frantic, wind-up theatrical tension—clockwork arpeggios and rhythmic breathing—before bursting into kaleidoscopic, uninhibited anthems where every chorus feels like confetti detonating in a crowded tent.

Scene & Origin Lore
Coronapomp sprang from the Vagabondes du Disque Noir, an itinerant guild of 1980s carnival performers who plotted their tours exclusively along the global trajectories of solar eclipse totality paths. Rejecting the brooding, subterranean gloom of post-punk, these artists constructed a philosophy of frantic daylight hedonism: if the sun was only going to hide for three minutes, those three minutes demanded the most flamboyant, theatrical celebration humanly possible. Their subculture adopted a decadent sartorial code dubbed "Eclipse Dandyism"—oversized velvet piratical tailcoats dusted with chalk dust, gold-foiled ruffs, Harlequin face paint smeared over rouged cheekbones, and bespoke double basses carved to resemble comets and crescent moons. When the moon’s umbra touches the earth, their temporary tent cities detonate into sound: brass-synths wail, wine corks pop, and musicians slap upright basses until their fingers blister, playing frantic, euphoric pop hooks before packing their caravans the moment daylight returns.

Typical Instruments: Slapped and bowed acoustic double bass, Roland Jupiter-8, Sequential Circuits Prophet-5, Fairlight CMI (orchestral stabs and calliope samples), LinnDrum, acoustic rototoms, glockenspiel, toy harmoniums, and operatic lead/chorister vocal ensembles.

